# Implementation Plan: Experiment B (3D Video Sequence Modeling & Temporal Transfer)

Experiment A established that static 2D spatial models (ResNet-50) achieve near-perfect in-domain performance (~99% on OUC-CGE, ~96% on COLER) but experience a severe performance drop (down to 52% and 29%) when transferred cross-domain due to static spatial domain shift.

**Experiment B** addresses the temporal dimension of video engagement recognition by transitioning from static keyframes to **3D spatiotemporal video sequence modeling**. We use 3D Convolutional Networks (such as `R(2+1)D-18` or `ResNet3D-18`) to model contiguous 16-frame temporal windows `[C, T, H, W] = [3, 16, 224, 224]` and evaluate whether temporal motion patterns reduce cross-dataset domain shift.

---

## 1. Experimental Settings Architecture

Experiment B follows the same 4-setting experimental matrix as Experiment A, adapted for 3D spatiotemporal sequence inputs:

| Setting ID | Experiment Description | Input Shape | Training Domain | Test Evaluation Domain | Primary Metric |
|---|---|---|---|---|---|
| **Setting 1** | In-Domain OUC-CGE 3D Model | `[3, 16, 224, 224]` | OUC-CGE | OUC-CGE (5-Fold CV) | Macro F1 & Accuracy |
| **Setting 2** | In-Domain COLER 3D Model | `[3, 16, 224, 224]` | COLER | COLER (5-Fold CV) | Macro F1 & Accuracy |
| **Setting 3** | Direct Temporal Transfer (OUC $\rightarrow$ COLER) | `[3, 16, 224, 224]` | OUC-CGE | COLER (Zero-Shot) | Transfer Macro F1 & QWK |
| **Setting 4** | Reverse Temporal Transfer (COLER $\rightarrow$ OUC) | `[3, 16, 224, 224]` | COLER | OUC-CGE (Zero-Shot) | Transfer Macro F1 & QWK |

